     1	#!/usr/bin/env python3
     2	"""await_release_gate.py — PLAN-166 W0 item 6 (F1): decision function that
     3	tells ``npm-publish.yml`` whether the ``release.yml`` **release-gate** job
     4	actually passed for *this* tag, at *this* commit, in *this* push.
     5	
     6	## The bug this closes (F1, P0)
     7	
     8	``npm-publish.yml`` and ``release.yml`` both fire on ``push: tags: v*``.
     9	They are INDEPENDENT runs — nothing made the publish observe the gate. The
    10	only barrier was the ``production-npm`` environment approval, i.e. a human
    11	clicking "approve" with no machine evidence that the governance gate was
    12	even green. That is a live path to publishing an unreviewed tree.
    13	
    14	## Contract
    15	
    16	Pure, offline, stdlib-only. Input is the JSON the caller already fetched
    17	(``gh api .../actions/runs?head_sha=...`` merged with each run's
    18	``.../jobs``); output is exactly one of three decisions **per evaluation
    19	point** (the caller polls; each poll is one independent decision):
    20	
    21	* ``GRANT`` — only when ONE run satisfies EVERY condition simultaneously:
    22	  workflow file ``release.yml``, ``event == "push"``,
    23	  ``head_branch == <tag>``, ``head_sha == <GITHUB_SHA>``, it is FRESH
    24	  (see below), and its **job** ``release-gate`` has
    25	  ``conclusion == "success"``. Never the RUN conclusion: ``release.yml``
    26	  carries ``if: vars.CEO_SOTA_DISABLE != '1'`` on ``release-gate``, so a
    27	  disabled gate SKIPS the job while the run itself stays green. Reading the
    28	  run conclusion would grant on a gate that never executed.
    29	
    30	* ``WAIT`` — evidence is legitimately not in yet and the deadline has not
    31	  passed: (a) no candidate run yet — workflows from the same push start in
    32	  ARBITRARY order, absence is neither failure nor permission; (b) the
    33	  candidate run exists but the ``release-gate`` job has not materialised in
    34	  the jobs endpoint yet (eventual consistency — without this state a
    35	  "BLOCK on mismatch" rule produces an instant false block in the rc.2/GA
    36	  race); (c) the job exists with ``conclusion: null`` (queued/running).
    37	
    38	* ``BLOCK`` — fail-CLOSED: the candidate's gate job concluded anything
    39	  other than ``success`` (``failure``, ``skipped``, ``cancelled``, …),
    40	  malformed JSON, an API error payload, or **the deadline elapsed in ANY
    41	  non-GRANT state**. Per ADR-186 this is INPUT verification, not
    42	  infrastructure: content we cannot verify is blocked, not waved through.
    43	
    44	## Candidate semantics (load-bearing — do not "optimise" this away)
    45	
    46	The head-SHA run list contains UNRELATED runs, **including the npm-publish
    47	run doing the asking**. Non-candidate runs are IGNORED — never BLOCK.
    48	"Mismatch" is only ever evaluated against the exact candidate
    49	(workflow + tag + SHA + event). If any near-miss run could BLOCK, every
    50	release would lose the race against its own presence in the list.
    51	
    52	## Freshness (delete + re-tag of the SAME sha)
    53	
    54	Re-tagging the same commit leaves the OLD Release run in the list with the
    55	same ``head_sha``/``head_branch``. Polling before the NEW Release run is
    56	created would otherwise find the old ``success`` as "most recent" and grant
    57	— even if the new run later fails. So a candidate must have been created no
    58	earlier than the asking run's own creation, minus ``--freshness-skew-seconds``
    59	(default 120s) to absorb same-push jitter: both workflows are created by one
    60	push event, and their ``created_at`` ordering is arbitrary within seconds.
    61	Runs older than that window are not candidates at all (→ WAIT, then BLOCK at
    62	the deadline). KNOWN LIMIT, stated rather than hidden: a delete+re-tag
    63	completed FASTER than the skew window can still admit the previous run; the
    64	skew is a jitter allowance, not a proof, and it is printed with every
    65	decision so the value used is auditable.
    66	
    67	``--self-created-at`` is REQUIRED. It is the input that switches this whole
    68	leg on, so it gets no default: omitting it (or passing an empty/unparseable
    69	value) is a usage error (exit 2), never a run that silently grants stale
    70	successes. Same doctrine as ``_release_bump_sites.py --today``: a parameter
    71	that changes the verdict has no default. The doctrine holds at BOTH layers:
    72	``GateContext.self_created_at_epoch`` is likewise a required field with no
    73	default (and an explicit ``None`` raises), so an in-process caller of
    74	``decide()`` cannot construct a context with the freshness leg silently off.
    75	
    76	## Required fields per run object
    77	
    78	``path`` (or ``workflow_path``), ``event``, ``head_branch``, ``head_sha``,
    79	``created_at``, optional ``run_attempt``/``id`` (tie-break), and ``jobs``
    80	(a list, or the raw ``{"jobs": [...]}`` envelope). A run with no ``path``
    81	cannot be attributed to a workflow and is therefore not a candidate.
    82	
    83	## Usage
    84	
    85	    python3 .claude/scripts/await_release_gate.py \
    86	        --payload-file runs.json --tag v1.3.0 --head-sha "$GITHUB_SHA" \
    87	        --self-created-at "$SELF_CREATED_AT" --deadline-epoch "$DEADLINE"
    88	
    89	``--payload-file -`` reads stdin.
    90	
    91	Exit codes:
    92	    0 — GRANT   (publish may proceed)
    93	    1 — BLOCK   (fail-closed; caller must fail the job)
    94	    2 — usage error (bad arguments)
    95	    3 — WAIT    (caller sleeps and polls again)
    96	"""
    97	
    98	from __future__ import annotations
    99	
