     1	# PLAN-158 Wave 1 — OIDC GA-publish failure playbook
     2	
     3	> **Why this exists (mandatory per the wave):** `npm-publish.yml` has **no
     4	> `workflow_dispatch`**, and a tag run pins the workflow to the tag's tree.
     5	> A failed OIDC-only GA publish therefore CANNOT be fixed by a re-run with
     6	> different auth — the only path is: apply the pre-staged rollback diff on
     7	> main, then **delete and re-tag** so the new run executes the rolled-back
     8	> tree. There is also **no earlier proof point**: RC tags skip
     9	> `npm-publish.yml` entirely (`!contains(github.ref, '-rc.')`), so the GA
    10	> publish is the first end-to-end OIDC exercise by design (debate: no RC
    11	> dist-tag publish to "prove OIDC early" — security guardrail, recorded).
    12	
    13	## Failure signatures at the Publish step
    14	
    15	| Signature | Meaning | Likely cause |
    16	|---|---|---|
    17	| `ENEEDAUTH` | npm found no usable credential | npm CLI < 11.5.1 (upgrade step failed/skipped) OR trusted publisher not registered on npmjs.com OR repo/workflow/environment mismatch in the registration |
    18	| `E403` / `E404` on publish | Exchange happened but registry refused | Trusted-publisher registration fields don't match (`Canhada-Labs/ceo-orchestration` + `npm-publish.yml` + env `production-npm` — the workflow FILENAME must match, not the display name) |
    19	| OIDC token fetch error | JWT never minted | `id-token: write` permission missing (should be impossible — it's in the workflow) or GitHub OIDC outage |
    20	
    21	**First move on any failure:** check the registration on
    22	npmjs.com → package `ceo-orchestration` → Settings → Trusted Publisher.
    23	Field mismatch is the dominant failure class; fixing the registration and
    24	re-running the SAME failed run (re-run executes the same tag tree — the
    25	OIDC tree — which is fine when the registration was the problem) is the
    26	cheapest recovery and needs **no rollback**.
    27	
    28	## Recovery A — registration fix (preferred; no rollback)
    29	
    30	1. Owner fixes the trusted-publisher fields on npmjs.com.
    31	2. Re-run the failed `NPM Publish` run (GitHub UI → Re-run failed jobs).
    32	3. The `already_published` guard keeps this idempotent.
    33	
    34	## Recovery B — rollback to token auth (last resort)
    35	
    36	Run from a clean main checkout, Owner terminal:
    37	
    38	```bash
    39	# 0. Regenerate a granular token FIRST (npmjs.com console — the old one
    40	#    may already be revoked/expired): scope = ceo-orchestration,
    41	#    publish permission. Store it as the repo secret NPM_TOKEN.
    42	
    43	# 1. Apply the pre-staged rollback diff (restores env: NODE_AUTH_TOKEN
    44	#    on the Publish step; nothing else changes):
    45	git apply .claude/plans/PLAN-158/staged/wave1/rollback-oidc-to-token.patch
    46	
    47	# 2. This edits a guarded workflow: land it under the SENT-OIDC sentinel's
    48	#    pre-authorized rollback clause (the sentinel Scope carries
    49	#    npm-publish.yml; the rollback diff is named IN the signed body), or
    50	#    sign a fresh sentinel if the original has been superseded.
    51	git add .github/workflows/npm-publish.yml
    52	git commit -m "revert(PLAN-158): rollback npm-publish to token auth (OIDC GA failure — playbook Recovery B)"
    53	git push origin main
    54	
    55	# 3. Delete the GA tag + release, re-tag on the rolled-back tree:
    56	gh release delete v1.1.0 --yes            # if the release was created
    57	git tag -d v1.1.0
    58	git push origin :refs/tags/v1.1.0
    59	git tag -s v1.1.0 -m "v1.1.0"             # signed, on the rolled-back HEAD
    60	git push origin v1.1.0
    61	
    62	# 4. Approve production-npm when prompted; publish runs with token auth.
    63	
    64	# 5. Re-date the expiry flags (the fallback obligation from OQ1):
    65	#    docs/PLAN-152-deferred-status.md + npm-publish.yml header +
    66	#    .github/workflows/GOVERNANCE-MAP.md:56 — new token expiry date.
    67	```
    68	
    69	> **Note:** deleting + re-creating a GA tag is itself a release-hygiene
    70	> event — record it in the plan §Clarifications with the reason. The
    71	> release.yml gates re-run on the new tag (pair-rail verdict is keyed on
    72	> the tag NAME, so the existing `pair-rail-verdict-v1.1.0.md` stays valid
    73	> if ≤24h old and the parent sha unchanged; otherwise regenerate).
    74	
    75	## Success path (what "done" means)
    76	
    77	1. `NPM Publish` green on the v1.1.0 GA tag; `npm view ceo-orchestration@1.1.0 version` prints `1.1.0`.
    78	2. Provenance visible on the npm package page (Sigstore, now attesting via trusted publishing).
    79	3. **Owner REVOKES the old granular NPM_TOKEN on npmjs.com** (not just stops using it) and deletes the `NPM_TOKEN` repo secret; revocation recorded in `docs/rotation-log.md`.
    80	4. `npx ceo-orchestration@latest --help` exits 0 (Wave 4 smoke).
